From bf9190be2173968c9a167708963089b43c915c65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 19:10:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Stop the video model re-acting the scene over a good keyframe
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The keyframes are fine. Measured on a real render: the first frame of
every scene is relaxed and natural, the eyes are already wide by 0.8s,
and full alarm holds from 1.6s to the end of the shot. The whole defect
lives in the image-to-video stage, which was being sent

    `${entry.visual}. ${motion}`

Two things wrong with that, neither of them the keyframe.

entry.visual is a description of a STILL, and the image model has already
rendered it into the keyframe being animated. Sending it again asks the
video model to perform the same beat a second time on top of a frame that
already contains it — so "alarmed by a shocking statistic" gets acted out,
having already been composed.

And `motion` is a camera note ("Subtle natural motion, cinematic"). Nothing
in the prompt constrained the face at all. Given seconds to fill and no
constraint, wan-2.2-i2v-fast falls into its presenter prior — brows up,
eyes wide — and nothing pulls it back.

A shot that will be lip-synced now sends no scene prose: its keyframe is
the scene, and the only thing left to ask for is that the person move like
a person while nothing else changes. Mouth acting there is thrown away by
LatentSync regardless; what survives to the finished video is the eyes and
brows, which is what had gone wrong. An unsynced shot keeps its prose and
gains the lighter guard, since it may be an action rather than a piece to
camera.

Both clauses speak only about the face, so a picked motion preset still
owns the camera and is never contradicted or dropped.

